From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: unom <packages@unom.io>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 09:33:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 15/33] pipewire: offer a P010 capture format (BT.2020 PQ,
 4:2:0)

An HDR consumer whose encoder takes 4:2:0 directly (VAAPI Main 10, Vulkan Video) had two 10-bit choices, both packed RGB, so it paid a colour conversion of its own on every frame after this compositor had already composited the picture once. The NV12 offer covers that for SDR; nothing covered it at ten bits.

P010 is NV12 with 16-bit samples, the 10-bit code in the high bits. The offer carries the same MANDATORY PQ + BT.2020 description as the packed 10-bit formats plus a MANDATORY BT.2020 limited-range matrix, so a consumer that wants anything else cannot land on it. The composite takes the HDR route the packed formats take, into the pooled 10-bit RGB texture, and a new pass writes the planes: the PQ codes go through the BT.2020 matrix as they are, never linearised, with the legal 10-bit range folded in. The planes are written through R16 / R16G16 views, so the image carries the mutable-format flag; the buffer geometry code treats NV12 and P010 as one planar family.

Listed last, after the packed 10-bit formats: a consumer that prefers it names it first in its own offer.

Stamp pfhdr13 so a host can tell the format is there before it commits a session.
